🛡️ Intelligent Web Application Firewall (WAF) with Machine Learning
An advanced Web Application Firewall that combines rule-based threat detection with machine learning capabilities to provide robust protection against modern web threats.

🚀 Features
Hybrid Detection Engine: Combines signature-based rules with ML analysis
Real-time Dashboard: Live monitoring and threat visualization
Multi-Threat Protection: Detects SQL Injection, XSS, and Command Injection
Machine Learning Integration: Extensible ML framework for advanced threat detection
Comprehensive Logging: Detailed request logging with threat classification
Performance Metrics: Real-time statistics and detection rates
🏗️ Architecture



intelligent-waf-ml/
├── src/
│   ├── waf_engine.py         # Main WAF engine
│   ├── data_collector.py     # Data collection and logging
│   ├── dashboard.py          # Real-time monitoring dashboard
│   └── ml_components/        # ML model components (placeholder)
├── logs/
│   └── waf_logs.db           # SQLite database for logging
├── config/
│   └── waf_config.json       # Configuration file
├── requirements.txt          # Python dependencies
└── README.md                 # This file
🛠️ Installation
bash



# Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/Ashel7577/intelligent-waf-ml.git
cd intelligent-waf-ml

# Install dependencies
pip install -r requirements.txt

# Create necessary directories
mkdir -p logs config
▶️ Usage
Starting the Dashboard
bash



python src/dashboard.py
Access the dashboard at http://localhost:8080

Integrating WAF into Applications
python



from src.waf_engine import IntelligentWAF

# Initialize WAF
waf = IntelligentWAF()

# Process incoming requests
request = {
    'ip_address': '192.168.1.100',
    'method': 'GET',
    'url': '/api/users',
    'body': ''
}

result = waf.process_request(request)
if result.blocked:
    print(f"Blocked threat: {result.threat_type}")
else:
    print("Request allowed")
🔍 Threat Detection Capabilities
Currently Supported Threats
SQL Injection: Pattern matching for common SQLi techniques
Cross-Site Scripting (XSS): Detection of script injection attempts
Command Injection: Identification of OS command execution attempts
Detection Methods
Rule-Based Engine: Fast pattern matching using regex signatures
Machine Learning Engine: Statistical analysis for anomaly detection (extensible)
📊 Dashboard Features
Real-time request monitoring
Threat distribution visualization
Performance metrics (detection rate, confidence scores)
Recent activity timeline
Automated refresh every 2 seconds
📈 Machine Learning Roadmap
This WAF is designed with ML extensibility in mind:

Feature extraction framework ready
Model integration points
Training data collection
Performance evaluation metrics
